WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.860
<v Instructor>So I want to show you</v>

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:03.450
how to use the EKS service.

3
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:04.860
You don't have to follow my hands-on,

4
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.350
it's just for you to get an idea of how this works

5
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.730
and how this gets configured

6
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.260
so you have a better understanding of the service,

7
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.060
but we're not going to use it

8
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.130
and it may cost you money to do so,

9
00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.990
so just look at what I'm doing.

10
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.940
Of course, if you want to, you can follow along.

11
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.500
So we are in the EKS console

12
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.930
and we're going to create a cluster.

13
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.260
So here we have two options, the quick configuration

14
00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.890
or the custom configuration to choose more options.

15
00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.840
So let's go over custom configuration

16
00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.110
and we'll have a look at all the options quickly.

17
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.220
So let's scroll down.

18
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.280
And now we have the EKS Auto Mode, which is new.

19
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.040
We're going to use it, this is going to make sure that

20
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.330
when a new pod,

21
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.910
so a new task, cannot fit onto existing nodes,

22
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.070
EKS will automatically create a new node for us.

23
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.410
That means that we have the infrastructure

24
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.100
that is managed by EKS, which is great.

25
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.173
So I'll call this one DemoEKSCluster.

26
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.980
Then there is a cluster role. So this is very important.

27
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.140
We currently don't have a role for this cluster.

28
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.630
So you click on create recommended role,

29
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.370
and this is going to automatically create the role

30
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.070
we need for this cluster.

31
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:12.330
So then it needs to also have these managed policy

32
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.580
or equivalent to use EKS Auto Mode.

33
00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.640
So I guess this is not created out of the box for us.

34
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.850
So in permission policies we would add policy,

35
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.750
attach policies, and then we would look for EKS,

36
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.610
and then we would need to match this.

37
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:31.610
So EKS block storage, cluster policy, compute policy,

38
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.420
and then load balancing and networking,

39
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.080
load balancing and networking.

40
00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:38.310
Add permissions.

41
00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.310
And now we have the proper permissions to this role.

42
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.890
Let's refresh this. No more error message.

43
00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.870
The Kubernetes version, we're good to go.

44
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.850
Standard support, yes.

45
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.600
We don't need to enable a higher scaling tier.

46
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.550
Auto Mode is going to say

47
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.070
what we choose the node pools to choose from.

48
00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.810
So general purpose and system.

49
00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:58.643
Node IAM role,

50
00:01:58.643 --> 00:02:00.450
this is the IAM role needed for the nodes

51
00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.100
to be registered with the cluster.

52
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.350
So there's a role here, but it's not the right one.

53
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.540
So we need to create a recommended role

54
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.690
called AmazonEKSAutoNodeRole.

55
00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.580
It has the default policies right here.

56
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.380
Let's create this role.

57
00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.780
And no error message. So we're good here.

58
00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.430
Let's remove these notifications.

59
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.820
Then we have the cluster access.

60
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.300
So we allow cluster administrator access

61
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.770
for your IAM principal

62
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.450
to make things easy.

63
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.040
We don't need to change anything here.

64
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.200
We don't need to look at encryption right now.

65
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.400
This is a way for you

66
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.240
to shift traffic across AZs in case there's a problem in AZ.

67
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.550
So for now we'll disable it, but we can enable it after.

68
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.430
This is just a way for you to have a better

69
00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.490
disaster recovery protection.

70
00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.740
Let's click on next. Networking is more interesting.

71
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.040
So you choose the VPC to launch your EKS resources in

72
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.910
as well as subnets where to launch your resources.

73
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.760
So here are three subnets that are public.

74
00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.130
You could add security groups if you wanted to,

75
00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.680
but right now we don't need to.

76
00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.210
Choose the type of IP you want to use.

77
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.533
IPv4 is fine.

78
00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.470
Then how to access the cluster.

79
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.870
Is it public, public and private or just private?

80
00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.373
We'll use public and private.

81
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.130
Next you have all the metrics.

82
00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.660
So you have metrics

83
00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.100
that are gonna go directly in CloudWatch,

84
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.010
but also you can have Prometheus if you wanted to.

85
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.003
To set these up.

86
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.480
You have network monitoring, control plane logs.

87
00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.250
So all these things are Kubernetes specific.

88
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.690
Again, I just wanna show you the options,

89
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.820
but you can see you can also send all the logs

90
00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.850
into CloudWatch logs for these things if you need to.

91
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.220
So it's quite a lot of options when you

92
00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.410
configure your EKS cluster.

93
00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.100
You can also add add-ons for your cluster.

94
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.700
This is Kubernetes-related knowledge, so I will not do it,

95
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.280
but as you can see, you have lots of options around DNS,

96
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.250
around SageMaker, around EFS and so on.

97
00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.183
So it's great.

98
00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.090
And then you also have community add-ons

99
00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.820
if you want around all these things.

100
00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.950
So let's scroll down.

101
00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.130
And then you have third party marketplace add-ons

102
00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.510
if you wanted to.

103
00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.590
Let's click on next, then this how you configure an add-on

104
00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.860
and then you review everything and you scroll down

105
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.810
and create your cluster.

106
00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.820
So if you're a Kubernetes expert, you would know

107
00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.310
that it's difficult to set up your own Kubernetes cluster,

108
00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.950
but thanks to AWS, it's managed

109
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:23.130
and it has the resources created for you behind the scenes

110
00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.643
and you make sure that the cluster is properly configured.

111
00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.940
So my EKS cluster is now created

112
00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.740
and I wanna show you a few things.

113
00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.900
First of all, under resources,

114
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.330
you have access to the resources within Kubernetes.

115
00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.270
So you have your pods in here, you have your replica sets,

116
00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.880
your deployments, your stateful sets and so on.

117
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.430
So all these things are Kubernetes specific,

118
00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.100
but you can see them directly from the UI

119
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.100
of the EKS service, which is very handy.

120
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.020
Under compute, this is where you're going to see your nodes.

121
00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.930
So this is a c6g.large

122
00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.970
that has been created thanks to the Auto Mode.

123
00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.040
So you can see it's a pretty large instance

124
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.150
and you would pay a lot of money

125
00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.470
if you were to do this on your account.

126
00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.150
So don't have to follow this hands-on

127
00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:04.890
if you don't want to, of course.

128
00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:07.290
But this is the built-in node pool.

129
00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.120
So this is managed by EKS Auto Mode.

130
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.220
But if you scroll down, you have two options.

131
00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.800
You have node groups and fargate profiles.

132
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.080
So I'll just show you how to create a node group

133
00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.430
if you needed to.

134
00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.120
This is a way for you to create a set of EC2 instances

135
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.250
that will be managed by an auto scaling group.

136
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:24.900
So I call this one DemoNodeGroup.

137
00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.720
You need to select a role.

138
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.540
So I click on create recommended role.

139
00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.430
I scroll down

140
00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.800
and the policies have been selected for me already,

141
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:35.940
so I don't need to do anything.

142
00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.833
Click on next and I'll call this one NodeGroupRoleEKS.

143
00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:43.980
As you can see, these four policies

144
00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:44.910
have been added automatically.

145
00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.200
So it's perfect.

146
00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.390
And we create this role.

147
00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.300
So now we get this role name, we enter it,

148
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.250
refresh and enter it.

149
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:54.083
Here we go.

150
00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.570
Next we can, for example, use a launch template.

151
00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:00.900
So this is to indicate how our EC2 instances

152
00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:05.100
are going to be launched within our auto scaling group.

153
00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.230
So you would go ahead and create a launch template,

154
00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.720
and this is all the information we already know

155
00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.290
around the AMIs, around the instance type that we want

156
00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:14.190
and so on.

157
00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:15.270
So we've seen this before,

158
00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.763
so I'm not going to go over it right now.

159
00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.820
Then click on next.

160
00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.300
So here you choose the AMI type, the capacity,

161
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.490
the instance types, the disc size,

162
00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.440
then the minimum and maximum size

163
00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.990
for your auto scaling group,

164
00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.090
whether or not you want to use a warm pool.

165
00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:36.330
I mean all these things are auto scaling group specific.

166
00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:39.360
And enable node repairs in case you have an issue.

167
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.660
Then you specify networking. So where to launch these nodes.

168
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.150
And then you would go ahead and create this.

169
00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:48.423
And this is going to create its own auto scaling group.

170
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.030
And so after a bit I can see that

171
00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.050
two nodes are appearing under this node group.

172
00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.830
And actually if I go into the EC2 console,

173
00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:01.800
within my instances, I can see that I have,

174
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.170
this is my management instance, the c6 large,

175
00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.750
and this is the two t3.medium instances.

176
00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:07.860
And so if I go on the left hand side

177
00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.450
under auto scaling groups,

178
00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.280
we can see that this auto scaling group

179
00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.600
was created by EKS

180
00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.090
and contains the configuration we just did.

181
00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:19.950
So we can see how EKS is now managing my node group.

182
00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:24.120
So I go back to resources, we would have a computes,

183
00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.730
we would have a similar type of configuration

184
00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.980
for fargate profiles in which we define

185
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.000
how to launch pods directly on fargates managed by EKS.

186
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.250
So I'm not gonna do this, but you get the idea.

187
00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.860
Under add-ons, I just wanna show you something.

188
00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.050
So you can click on get on more add-ons,

189
00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:44.610
and you can look at something called the EBS CSI driver.

190
00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.910
And if you add it as well as the EFS CSI driver,

191
00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.610
this would allow you to use EBS volumes

192
00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:54.840
or Amazon EFS file systems within your cluster.

193
00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.810
And this is something that is important to know,

194
00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:59.340
but I won't do this right now.

195
00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:00.780
So that's it, I just wanted to show you

196
00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.760
how to configure your cluster.

197
00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.320
Now I will delete it.

198
00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:05.850
So to do so, I need to make sure

199
00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:07.650
I first delete my node groups and the cluster,

200
00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.150
so I'm going do this offline.

201
00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.750
But you get the idea of how EKS works now in AWS.

202
00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:13.950
So I hope you liked it

203
00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.903
and I will see you in the next lecture.

